# 📊 Math Modeling Viz

**Publication-quality charts for mathematical modeling papers — one import, one function call, done.**

[![Python 3.9+](https://img.shields.io/badge/python-3.9%2B-blue.svg)](https://www.python.org/downloads/)
[![License: MIT](https://img.shields.io/badge/License-MIT-yellow.svg)](LICENSE)
[![CI](https://github.com/shipnebula/Modeling_Drawing_Skill/actions/workflows/ci.yml/badge.svg)](../../actions/workflows/ci.yml)
[![Code style: black](https://img.shields.io/badge/code%20style-black-000000.svg)](https://github.com/psf/black)
[![PRs Welcome](https://img.shields.io/badge/PRs-welcome-brightgreen.svg)](CONTRIBUTING.md)

[English](#-features) | [中文文档](#-中文说明)

---

Math Modeling Viz turns the chart-making part of **CUMCM / MCM-ICM / academic papers** into a one-liner. Instead of fighting matplotlib for an afternoon, you describe the data and get a Nature-style figure with correct fonts, spines, grid, palette, and export settings.

```python
from plot import plot

fig = plot([120, 180, 150], labels=['2022', '2023', '2024'], title='Revenue')
fig.savefig('revenue.pdf')   # vector PDF, ready for submission
```

<p align="center">
  <img src="docs/images/banner.png" width="860" alt="Math Modeling Viz gallery banner">
</p>

## ✨ Features

- **🎯 155+ chart types** — from bar/line/pie to 3-D surfaces, vector fields, phase fields with integrated orbits, parameter sweeps, bifurcation diagrams, Monte-Carlo convergence, correlograms, ternary diagrams, chord diagrams, forest plots, volcano plots, risk matrices, beeswarms, rainclouds, circle packing, SPC control charts, and calculus visuals.
- **🤖 Smart auto-detection** — pass a list, dict, NumPy array, or DataFrame and `plot()` picks a sensible chart type; fuzzy type hints (`'barr'` → *"did you mean 'bar'?"*) catch typos.
- **🎨 30+ curated palettes** — colorblind-safe (Paul Tol, Okabe-Ito), Nature/Science-inspired, sequential, diverging, dark-mode. Picked by analysts, not by `plt.cm.viridis` roulette.
- **🖌 7 style presets** — `nature`, `publication`, `economist`, `minimal`, `dark`, `poster`, `dark_poster`. One call, consistent typography.
- **🀄 CJK support** — Chinese/Japanese/Korean labels render correctly on Windows, macOS, and Linux out of the box (fonts auto-detected and applied at import).
- **🧩 Multi-panel figures** — `panel_figure()` composes grids of charts with automatic **(a), (b), (c)** labels in Nature style.
- **⚡ Fast** — min/max decimation keeps 200k-point line charts snappy; vectorized colormaps; cached styling; headless-safe backend detection for servers and CI.
- **⌨️ CLI included** — `mmv plot data.csv -t bar -o out.pdf` renders charts straight from the terminal.
- **🎬 GIF export** — `save_gif(figures, 'demo.gif')` animates convergence and parameter sweeps with zero extra dependencies.
- **♿ Colorblind tooling** — `cvd_preview()` renders your palette under protanopia / deuteranopia / tritanopia simulations; `colorblind_safe()` quantifies it.
- **🎨 `style()` context manager** — temporarily switch looks (`with style("dark"):`) with automatic restore; `export_publication()` writes PNG+PDF+SVG in one call.
- **⏱ Instant CLI previews** — `mmv preview radar` renders a demo of any chart type in one command.
- **📐 Declarative rendering** — `render_spec()` / `mmv spec recipe.json` drive every chart from a JSON recipe — ideal for LLM pipelines and config-driven reports.
- **🎨 Theme presets** — `apply_theme('colorblind')` bundles style + palette + accessibility in one call (11 themes).
- **🩺 `mmv doctor`** — one-command environment health check (deps, versions, CJK fonts, chart registry).
- **📑 One-call appendix PDF** — `pdf_report([figs], 'appendix.pdf', title=...)` builds a multi-page, cover-titled submission document (CUMCM 支撑材料).
- **⚙️ Project-local defaults** — drop a `.mmvrc.json` next to your script (`{"theme": "colorblind", "dpi": 200}`) and every figure follows it — zero code.
- **🎞 `make_gif()`** — animate any figure sequence from one frame function; the README banner is generated this way.
- **🌐 Browsable gallery + docs site** — `docs/gallery.html` regenerates alongside the images; an MkDocs Material site (`mkdocs serve`) publishes the full documentation.
- **⚙️ Workflow CLI** — `mmv init` writes a starter recipe; `mmv batch specs/` renders a whole directory of JSON recipes in one command.
- **🔤 LaTeX toggle** — `with use_latex():` renders titles and labels with real LaTeX (mathtext fallback built in).
- **📊 Model comparison suite** — `fit_comparison` (linear/poly/power/exp ranked by R²), `roc_comparison` (multi-model overlay), calibration & gain/lift charts, Bland–Altman agreement.
- **🧮 Calculus & statistics visuals** — definite integrals, Riemann sums, tangent lines, hypothesis-test rejection regions, Lorenz/Gini curves, and time-series forecast bands.
- **📦 Zero config** — every figure returns a plain `matplotlib.figure.Figure`, so anything matplotlib can do still works.

## 🚀 Quick start

```bash
pip install -r requirements.txt
```

> Requires Python 3.9+, matplotlib ≥ 3.7, numpy ≥ 1.24, pandas ≥ 2.0, scipy ≥ 1.10.
> Optional: seaborn, networkx, scikit-learn unlock a few advanced charts.

### The universal `plot()` function

```python
from plot import plot

# 1. Bar chart (auto-detected)
plot([10, 20, 30], labels=['A', 'B', 'C'], title='Results')

# 2. Multi-line convergence plot
plot({'GA': [0.6, 0.8, 0.9], 'SA': [0.62, 0.84, 0.95]}, x=range(3), title='Convergence')

# 3. Scatter with fit and confidence band
plot(x, y, type='scatter', show_fit=True)

# 4. Sensitivity analysis (tornado)
plot(parameters=['Price', 'Demand', 'Cost'], low_values=[...], high_values=[...],
     type='sensitivity', title='Sensitivity')

# 5. Correlation heatmap straight from a DataFrame
plot(df, type='correlation')

# 6. Nature-style multi-panel figure with (a)(b)(c) labels
from plot import panel_figure
panel_figure([
    {'type': 'bar',  'data': [4, 7, 5],  'labels': ['A', 'B', 'C'], 'title': 'Accuracy'},
    {'type': 'line', 'data': {'M1': [1, 2, 3]}, 'x': [1, 2, 3], 'title': 'Loss'},
    {'type': 'hist', 'data': residuals, 'title': 'Residuals'},
    {'type': 'radar', 'data': ['Speed', 'Cost', 'Robustness'],
     'args': [[[4, 3, 5]]], 'names': ['Model'], 'title': 'Profile'},
], ncols=2, suptitle='Figure 1: Overall results')
```

### Batch generation with `FigureFactory`

```python
from plot import FigureFactory

f = FigureFactory(style='publication', title_prefix='Figure ', output_format='pdf')
f.bar([10, 20, 30], labels=['A', 'B', 'C'], title='Sales')
f.line({'Trend': [1, 2, 3, 4]}, x=[1, 2, 3, 4], title='Growth')
f.sensitivity(params, low, high, title='Sensitivity')
f.save_all('output/')          # → output/figure_01.pdf, figure_02.pdf, ...
```

### Command line

```bash
mmv plot data.csv --type bar --title "Results" -o output.pdf
mmv types                  # list all chart types
mmv palettes               # palette gallery with color swatches
mmv detect data.csv        # what would auto-detection pick?
mmv spec recipe.json       # render a figure from a JSON recipe
```

